    "result_text": "The full suite passes \u2014 there's a single test class (`AcceptanceTest`, 3 tests), all green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization for owner creation.\n\n**New file \u2014 `AddressNormalizer.java`** (`rest/validation/`, mirroring the existing `TelephoneNormalizer`): a Spring `@Component` whose `normalize(String)` method trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases (via `Locale.ROOT`), and expands whole-word street abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). Abbreviation matching is token-based, so a word like `STREET` isn't mangled. `null` stays `null`; whitespace-only collapses to `\"\"`.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**: injected the normalizer and, as the first step of `addOwner`, replaced the DTO's address with its normalized form:\n```java\nownerFieldsDto.setAddress(addressNormalizer.normalize(ownerFieldsDto.getAddress()));\n```\nBecause this runs before everything else, the change flows through automatically:\n- The **required-field check** (`rejectBlankOwnerFields`) now sees the normalized value, so `\"   \"` becomes `\"\"` and is rejected as blank (400).\n- **Household duplicate detection** (`rejectDuplicateHousehold`) and the **shared household id** (`householdId`) both read the now-normalized address, so all address comparisons use the normalized form. Existing owners were created through the same path, so their stored addresses are already normalized \u2014 comparisons stay consistent.\n- The stored/returned `address` is the normalized value.\n\n`updateOwner` is deliberately untouched, since the spec scopes normalization to owner *creation*.\n\nAll three acceptance tests (whitespace/case normalization, abbreviation expansion, blank-after-normalize rejection) pass, and the build is green.",
